
SUppose we have this dependency:

#+BEGIN_SRC grooy 
dependencies {
    implementation 'com.google.code.gson:gson:2.11.0'
}
#+END_SRC

* Finding the URL

This is a Maven-style dependency coordinate:
Understanding the coordinates

Group = com.google.code.gson

Identifies the organization or project namespace.

Artifact = gson

Identifies the particular library.

Version = 2.11.0

Selects the published version.

Gradle commonly looks in repositories such as Maven Central. The
same coordinates tell us where to look manually. 

For Maven Central, the dots in the group ID become directory
separators. The repository path is:
com/google/code/gson/gson/2.11.0/

The base repository URL is:

https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/

The artifact's JAR can therefore be downloaded directly from:

https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/com/google/code/gson/gson/2.11.0/gson-2.11.0.jar

That is the first important skill: translate the Gradle
coordinates into a Maven repository path. 

* Download the JAR and its POM
The POM (Project Object Model) is an XML file containing metadata about the library, including its dependencies.

Create a directory for our downloaded library:

#+BEGIN_SRC 
mkdir -p ~/apk-camp/libs/gson
cd ~/apk-camp/libs/gson
#+END_SRC

Download the JAR and POM with curl:

#+BEGIN_SRC 
BASE=https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/com/google/code/gson/gson/2.11.0

curl -fLO "$BASE/gson-2.11.0.jar"
curl -fLO "$BASE/gson-2.11.0.pom"

#+END_SRC

You can read the downloaded POM file:

#+BEGIN_SRC 
less gson-2.11.0.pom
#+END_SRC

or extract its dependencies:

#+BEGIN_SRC 
grep -A 6 '<dependencies>' gson-2.11.0.pom
#+END_SRC

The POM is the library's dependency map, although interpreting it
fully sometimes requires looking at parent POMs and other
metadata too. Maven documents this repository layout and the
meaning of artifact coordinates in its repository-layout guide,
at https://maven.apache.org/repositories/layout.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com

Not all dependencies need to be downloaded. These come in
different flavours:

- compile — generally relevant to the library's consumers.
- runtime — needed at runtime, even if not needed to compile your own source.
- test — for testing the library itself; normally not needed in your APK.
- provided — expected to be supplied by the environment, depending on the build system and use case.
- optional — not automatically inherited by consumers in the same way as ordinary dependencies.
